WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.480
 Hello and welcome to another walkthrough for Computer Architecture for Everybody.

00:00:04.480 --> 00:00:12.000
 We're playing with our 6504 emulator programming and kind of working through some increasingly complex samples as we go.

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:14.560
 So we're going to go into the uppercase sample.

00:00:14.560 --> 00:00:18.720
 Now, the previous thing we did was a loop and this is really more of an if test.

00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:23.680
 We're not going to do a loop and the next thing we're going to have an if test and a loop.

00:00:23.680 --> 00:00:27.440
 So we're going to, we're not going to store any data here.

00:00:27.440 --> 00:00:29.360
 It's really simple. We're just doing an if statement.

00:00:29.360 --> 00:00:32.080
 We're going to check in this one, hard coded valuables.

00:00:32.080 --> 00:00:36.880
 We're going to check to see if p is greater than or less than a.

00:00:36.880 --> 00:00:40.720
 So we're going to load into a the constant p.

00:00:40.720 --> 00:00:44.320
 We're going to compare the constant a with the accumulator.

00:00:44.320 --> 00:00:47.040
 We're doing a convert to uppercase here.

00:00:47.040 --> 00:00:53.840
 If it's already uppercase, we're going to skip down and if it's not uppercase, we're going to convert it to uppercase.

00:00:53.840 --> 00:00:57.840
 And this will only work for lowercase a through z, but you get the idea.

00:00:57.840 --> 00:01:01.360
 Okay, so let's take a look here, convert to uppercase.

00:01:01.360 --> 00:01:05.120
 So let's go ahead and so if we look at these things, we got a load.

00:01:05.120 --> 00:01:06.400
 We got our immediate.

00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:07.600
 So that's the letter p.

00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:11.760
 We got our compare and then we have our immediate, which is the letter a.

00:01:11.760 --> 00:01:19.760
 And then we have the branch of minus down two and the branch of minus is really skipping

00:01:19.760 --> 00:01:22.880
 this subtraction and going all the way to eight.

00:01:22.880 --> 00:01:31.200
 So the if then else that we've kind of got here is if it's minus, we're going to skip down two instructions to eight.

00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:39.440
 If it is not minus, we're going to run six and then that's going to be a subtract immediate of zero x 20.

00:01:39.440 --> 00:01:45.840
 Hopefully you kind of see that that's a two and that's a zero because the zero x 20.

00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:47.920
 You can see those at some point.

00:01:47.920 --> 00:01:49.680
 You should start seeing the hex.

00:01:49.680 --> 00:01:56.320
 Okay, let's go ahead and just run this at full speed and maybe I'll talk a little bit about it afterwards.

00:01:56.320 --> 00:01:58.960
 I'm just going to turn on narration and fire.

00:01:58.960 --> 00:01:59.920
 Oh, no, I'm going to step.

00:01:59.920 --> 00:02:00.480
 I'm going to step.

00:02:00.480 --> 00:02:03.120
 So let's do the first one.

00:02:03.120 --> 00:02:06.560
 Let's load the letter p into the accumulator.

00:02:06.560 --> 00:02:12.880
 PC zero x zero zero load accumulator immediate zero x seven zero.

00:02:12.880 --> 00:02:16.400
 So that's a zero x seven zero, which is lowercase p and ASCII.

00:02:16.400 --> 00:02:16.960
 Oh, hang on.

00:02:16.960 --> 00:02:19.200
 Let me bring up the ASCII chart, right?

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:20.880
 So now we can bring up the ASCII chart.

00:02:20.880 --> 00:02:22.160
 So that's kind of fun.

00:02:22.160 --> 00:02:23.920
 So I got this little ASCII chart for you.

00:02:23.920 --> 00:02:25.120
 Quick computation.

00:02:25.120 --> 00:02:30.320
 So zero x seven zero is a hopefully a lowercase p.

00:02:30.320 --> 00:02:30.880
 Look at that.

00:02:30.880 --> 00:02:33.280
 So zero x seven zero is a lowercase p.

00:02:33.280 --> 00:02:39.760
 The assembler converted this from a quote p quote, which is one of its many load accumulator

00:02:39.760 --> 00:02:46.000
 syntaxes and it changed it into zero one one one zero zero zero and which is the letter p.

00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:49.040
 Now we're going to compare this with the letter a.

00:02:49.040 --> 00:02:49.920
 So where is that?

00:02:49.920 --> 00:02:53.600
 We're instruction two now and we're going to compare it with the a.

00:02:53.600 --> 00:02:54.480
 And a is just

00:02:54.480 --> 00:02:59.600
 61 hex and there it is.

00:02:59.600 --> 00:03:00.880
 It's 61 hex.

00:03:00.880 --> 00:03:02.480
 And so let's just compare it.

00:03:02.480 --> 00:03:04.960
 Run one instruction.

00:03:04.960 --> 00:03:10.960
 PC zero x zero two compare accumulator with immediate zero x six one.

00:03:11.600 --> 00:03:22.320
 OK, so now we basically have it set so that p, which was in the accumulator, is greater than a.

00:03:22.320 --> 00:03:25.520
 We didn't set the zero flag and we didn't set the negative flag.

00:03:25.520 --> 00:03:34.800
 OK, and if this character that we just compared is less than a, then we're going to skip.

00:03:34.800 --> 00:03:37.520
 If it's not, we're going to subtract 20 from it.

00:03:37.520 --> 00:03:39.120
 So it's not going to be very exciting.

00:03:39.680 --> 00:03:45.200
 This is going to become zero x 50 because we're going to subtract zero x 20.

00:03:45.200 --> 00:03:46.880
 So let's go ahead and run that step.

00:03:46.880 --> 00:03:55.120
 Branch didn't get taken.

00:03:55.120 --> 00:03:58.480
 It fell right through into position zero x zero six.

00:03:58.480 --> 00:04:00.000
 And now we're going to do a subtract.

00:04:07.200 --> 00:04:12.160
 So now the next instruction is to stop, but you'll notice that we did subtract that 70.

00:04:12.160 --> 00:04:16.800
 The accumulator was 70, which is the letter where were we the letter p.

00:04:16.800 --> 00:04:23.200
 And we subtracted 20 hex to it, which just happens to be uppercase p.

00:04:23.200 --> 00:04:26.720
 So this was a convert to uppercase operation.

00:04:26.720 --> 00:04:33.920
 And the last step is just to stop PC zero x zero eight break no printed output.

00:04:33.920 --> 00:04:35.680
 So we didn't store anything.

00:04:35.680 --> 00:04:38.480
 We just are showing a simple if then else,

00:04:38.480 --> 00:04:43.840
 which turns into a sort of compare and skip based on flag values.

00:04:43.840 --> 00:04:48.880
 OK, so I hope you found this particular useful in the walkthrough.

00:04:48.880 --> 00:04:58.160
 And cheers and see you on the net.

